[{ "action": "append", "id": "event-modals", "value" : "<div tabindex=\"-1\" id=\"modal-3657bdf5-6353-4682-ab87-cd74fc5e831c\" class=\"modal\"><div class=\"modal-dialog modal-lg\"><div class=\"modal-content\"><div class=\"modal-header\"><a data-dismiss=\"modal\" class=\"close\"><span class=\"glyphicon glyphicon-remove\"></span></a><p class=\"text-muted\"><span class=\"glyphicon glyphicon-road\"></span> Research Papers</p></div><div class=\"modal-body\"><div class=\"bg-primary event-title\"><h4><span data-event-star=\"3657bdf5-6353-4682-ab87-cd74fc5e831c\" title=\"Add event to your program\"><span class=\"text-disabled glyphicon glyphicon-star-empty\"></span></span><strong>WebCQ: Cooperative Multi-agent Deep Reinforcement Learning for Scalable Web GUI Testing</strong></h4></div><div class=\"bg-info event-description\"><p>Multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL)-based techniques have shown promise for GUI testing.<br>\nHowever, as the complexity of modern GUI software increases, existing MARL-based approaches (e.g., \\textit{MARG} and \\textit{Fastbot}) struggle to scale due to the inherent limitations of their underlying tabular reinforcement learning algorithms.<br>\nThis limits their applicability to large-scale commercial GUI software, especially web applications with vast state spaces and many interactive elements.<br>\nTo fill this gap, we propose \\textit{WebCQ}, a novel MARL-based approach for scalable web GUI testing.<br>\n\\textit{WebCQ} incorporates QTRAN for multi-agent coordination and a lightweight synchronization mechanism, allowing it to work under asynchronous web testing scenarios.<br>\nIt extracts semantic and exploration features for each UI event to form an action vector.<br>\nThis vector is concatenated with the current state vector and fed into the policy network, enabling DQN-based decision making within a dynamic action space.<br>\nWe evaluated \\textit{WebCQ} on eight large-scale commercial websites.<br>\nUnder the same time budget and agent count, \\textit{WebCQ} explored 33.3% more states and executed 42.2% more unique actions than \\textit{MARG}, while triggering more failures on six of the eight websites under test.<br>\nIt also demonstrated strong scalability, maintaining higher action throughput during 20-hour experiments, and achieving greater performance improvements as the number of agents increased.<br>\nThese results show that \\textit{WebCQ} overcomes key limitations of existing MARL-based approaches, providing a scalable and effective solution to enhance modern web GUI testing.</p>\n<p></p><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1145/3832783.3834374\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"publication-link navigate\"><span class=\"glyphicon glyphicon-link\"></span> DOI</a><p></p><div class=\"row\"><div class=\"col-sm-6\"><a href=\"https://conf.researchr.org/profile/ase-2026/yujiafan\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"navigate\"><div class=\"media\"><span class=\"pull-left\"><img alt=\"micro-avatar\" src=\"https://conf.researchr.org/getDefaultImage/micro-avatar?1783985080000\" class=\"outputimage media-object thumbnail no-bottom-margin\"/></span><div class=\"media-body\"><h5 class=\"media-heading\">Yujia Fan<span class=\"pull-right\"><small></small></span></h5><h5 class=\"media-heading\"><span class=\"text-black\">Southern University of Science and Technology</span></h5><h5 class=\"media-heading\"><small>China</small></h5></div></div></a></div><div class=\"col-sm-6\"><a href=\"https://conf.researchr.org/profile/ase-2026/sinanwang\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"navigate\"><div class=\"media\"><span class=\"pull-left\"><img alt=\"Sinan Wang\" src=\"https://conf.researchr.org/getProfileImage/sinanwang/60f4b764-5390-4ddf-9620-ef085f2b4a56/micro-avatar?1785329016000\" class=\"outputimage media-object thumbnail no-bottom-margin\"/></span><div class=\"media-body\"><h5 class=\"media-heading\">Sinan Wang<span class=\"pull-right\"><small></small></span></h5><h5 class=\"media-heading\"><span class=\"text-black\">Southern University of